            r"Divide \(f(x)\) by \(x-a\). The remainder is constant, and evaluating at \(x=a\) shows that it equals \(f(a)\). Thus \(f(a)=0\) if and only if \(x-a\) divides \(f(x)\). It follows that a nonzero polynomial of degree \(r\) has at most \(r\) distinct roots: after removing the factor \(x-a\), any other root \(b\ne a\) must be a root of the quotient, since \(b-a\ne0\) is invertible. Induct on the degree. To interpolate values \(y_1,\ldots,y_s\) at distinct points \(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_s\), define \(L_i(x)=\prod_{j\ne i}\frac{x-\alpha_j}{\alpha_i-\alpha_j}\). The polynomial \(L_i\) equals \(1\) at \(\alpha_i\) and \(0\) at the other points, so \(f(x)=\sum_{i=1}^s y_iL_i(x)\) takes the required values and has degree less than \(s\). It is unique: the difference of two polynomials of degree less than \(s\) agreeing at all \(s\) points would have \(s\) roots and must be zero. For example, over \(\mathbb{F}_5\), the polynomial taking value \(2\) at \(0\) and \(4\) at \(1\) is \(f(x)=2(1-x)+4x=2+2x\). The formal derivative is \(f'(x)=\sum_i i a_i x^{i-1}\), with integers interpreted in the field; in characteristic \(p\), for example, \((x^p)'=0\). In a splitting field, \(f\) has repeated roots exactly when \(\operatorname{gcd}(f,f')\ne1\). A splitting field is an extension in which the polynomial factors completely into linear factors; its roots need not already lie in \(F\).",
