            r"A ring lets us add, subtract, and multiply according to precise rules. Here we consider commutative rings with identity \(1\): under addition they form an abelian group; multiplication is associative and commutative, has identity \(1\), and distributes over addition: \(a(b+c)=ab+ac\). Unlike a field, a ring need not make every nonzero element invertible. A zero divisor occurs when two nonzero elements \(a,b\) have product zero: \(ab=0\). An ideal \(I\subseteq R\) is an additive subgroup stable under multiplication by elements of \(R\): if \(r\in R\) and \(a\in I\), then \(ra\in I\). An ideal need not contain \(1\); if it did, it would contain every \(r=r\cdot1\) and would equal all of \(R\).",
